# Daily order summary

`orders.jsonl` contains one JSON object per line, one per order, with the fields `order_id`, `product`, `quantity`, and `unit_price`.

Write `summary.json` containing a single JSON object with exactly these keys:

- `orders`: the number of orders (an integer);
- `units`: the total quantity over all orders (an integer);
- `revenue`: the sum of quantity × unit_price over all orders, rounded to 2 decimal places (a number);
- `top_product`: the product with the largest total quantity (a string; there is no tie in this data).

# Export users as JSON Lines

The CRM import job reads JSON Lines. Convert `users.csv` (columns `id,first_name,last_name,email,signup_date`) into `users.jsonl`: one JSON object per line, one line per CSV row, in the same order. Each object has exactly these keys:

- `id`: the id as an integer;
- `name`: the first name and last name separated by a single space;
- `email`: the email address converted to lowercase;
- `signup`: the signup date as `YYYY-MM-DD` (the CSV writes dates as `MM/DD/YYYY`).

